A||||13||Rick 88||POP Fiero!||||5||
Z||000000||Rick 88||08-22-2001||01:27 AM||||Ok 88 experts, heres one for you. My GT just started making a popping noise coming from the right front suspension on rebound only.<BR>A visual check showed the sway bar and shock bolts are tight. My car has 70k miles and no accident damage. Any ideas on what it could be?<P>TIA<BR>Rick<BR>yellow 88 GT 5-speed<BR>(with optional POP)<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000001||mrfiero||08-22-2001||01:31 AM||||It is likely your rack bushing, although my GT makes weird noises every now and then.<P>Crawl under your GT and grab the tie rod shaft and try to wiggle it back and forth & up and down, etc.  It should not move at all.  Any movement means a worn bushing.  Also try pulling the tie rod towards the wheel (like trying to pop it out of its socket).  Again, any movement is bad.<P>GM no longer makes/stocks the factory plastic bushings, but Rodney Dickman sells a replacement for $19.  His website is:<BR> <A HREF="http://www.rodneydickman.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.rodneydickman.com</A>   <P><BR>It could also be bad lower balljoints.....inspect everything and go from there.<p>[This message has been edited by mrfiero (edited 08-22-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||lawrence||08-22-2001||12:13 PM||||It could also be the shock.  Make sure the piston nut (the one on top) is tight because if it isn't, the piston rod will rattle in the hole.<P>Good luck,<P>-L<BR> <A HREF="http://members.nccw.net/webe/products" TARGET=_blank>http://members.nccw.net/webe/products</A>  - WebElectric Products||||reg||1
Z||000003||skitime||08-22-2001||01:41 PM||||I had a noise like that on Veronica's car and it was the lower ball joint loose were the ball joint is pressed on the a-arm.<P>------------------<BR><UL TYPE=SQUARE><BR><LI>Red 88 GT T-TOP<BR><LI>Red 86 GT<BR></UL> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/signature-1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||DKOV||08-22-2001||02:25 PM||||I agree with Skitime...<P>Just had that problem fixed on my 88GT and it was the lower Ball Joint on the Driver's side front.<P>Needed completely replaced.  Once it was done, no more "bumping - popping" sound.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>DKOV -||||reg||1
Z||000005||Indiana_resto_guy||08-22-2001||03:08 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by lawrence:<BR><B>It could also be the shock.  Make sure the piston nut (the one on top) is tight because if it isn't, the piston rod will rattle in the hole.<P>Good luck,<P>-L<BR> <A HREF="http://members.nccw.net/webe/products" TARGET=_blank>http://members.nccw.net/webe/products</A>  - WebElectric Products</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>?<P>Nut on top? <BR>Top is held on by two bolts! <P>Rattle in hole?<BR>No hole to rattle in on top!<P>Lower or upper bushing in both shock and control arms OK?<P>||||reg||5
Z||000006||Rick 88||08-22-2001||04:37 PM||||I've already checked the shocks and swaybar links. They are fine. I am going to check the steering rack and tie rod. How can you check if the ball joints are loose?<P>Thanks,<BR>Rick 88||||reg||5
Z||000007||mrfiero||08-22-2001||05:34 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Indiana_resto_guy:<BR><B> ?<P>Nut on top? <BR>Top is held on by two bolts! <P>Rattle in hole?<BR>No hole to rattle in on top!<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>Keith,<P>'88 Fieros have different shocks than the '84-'87's.  '88s have a single nut on top of the piston rod that holds them to the frame (they are inside the coils and the top sticks through a "hat").  It is possible for this nut to be loose causing a rattle.<P>'84-'87s have 2 bolts on the top an bottom and are next to the spring (not inside). <P><BR>Take a look at one of your '88s and you'll see the difference.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>BTW......I got your phone message....I'm sending out the title and sub box tomorrow, so you'll have them next week at the latest.  E-mail me if there's anything else I need to send.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000008||mrfiero||08-22-2001||05:41 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Rick 88:<BR><B>I've already checked the shocks and swaybar links. They are fine. I am going to check the steering rack and tie rod. How can you check if the ball joints are loose?<P>Thanks,<BR>Rick 88</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>Jack up the front end, lock the steering column and have a friend grab the wheel at the 3 & 9 o'clock position.  Have him try wiggling the wheel while you are underneath the car looking at the lower balljoint, upper balljoint, tie rod and upper control arm shaft.<P>Sometimes the nuts on the upper shaft back off a hair allowing some slop in the upper bushings.  If you can spin/move the big washers on the upper shaft then you need to tighten the nuts down again.  I use an impact wrench, but a big breaker bar should work.<P>If you observe any movement at the balljoint while your friend is moving the wheel then you need a new balljoint.....same with the upper balljoint and the upper bushings (you don;t necessarily need to replace the bushings though).  <P>I highly recommend replacing balljonts in pairs......one might still be good, but it will likely fail soon and you'll just be wasting money on another alignment.<P>Good luck and let us know what you find out.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Rick 88||08-23-2001||01:46 AM||||Thanks for all the tips guys! I am going to get my buddy to help me check the ball joints. I don't think it is too bad yet as the popping is intermittent. I'll let you know what I found out.<P>Rick<BR>yellow 88 GT 5-speed||||reg||1
Z||000010||FieroLisa||08-23-2001||02:17 PM||||I had a driver side popping noise on my 86 coupe- it was a ball joint  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000011||Rick 88||08-24-2001||10:55 AM||||Thanks everyone!<P>Turns out I was lucky. It was the shock that was loose afterall. I just had to have the suspension loaded to see it! Now I want to check my wheel bearings.<P>Rick <BR>yellow 88 GT 5-speed<BR>||||reg||10
Z||000012||rodmcneill||08-24-2001||11:45 PM||||Let's hope it is not your wheel bearings. The fronts are not available for 88's unless someone starts making them again.||||reg||1
Z||000013||mrfiero||08-24-2001||11:51 PM||||I doubt your wheel bearings are bad......you only have 70K miles on them.  Of the many '88 Fieros I have owned I only had one wheel bearing go south on me and it had 145K miles.<P>The sealed bearings, while expensove to replace, last a LONG time.<BR> <BR>While new front bearing are no longer available there are plenty of good used ones out there if the need ever arises. <BR>  ||||reg||1
